# Copy to `.env.production` before `npm run build`.
#
# The client talks to the API directly, on the API's own host, so these are
# absolute. That is deliberate: the front end does not depend on its own web
# server proxying anything, and moving it to another server later is a deploy
# change rather than a code change.
#
# What makes a cross-origin call legal is the API's own allow-list.
# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` is returned only for origins it knows, and
# `https://fh.somuch-system.ru` is currently the only one. A front end served
# from anywhere else is refused by the browser before the request is even sent,
# and nothing in this file can change that — the origin has to be added on the
# server first. Check it with:
#
#   curl -s -i -H "Origin: https://your-origin" \
#     https://api-fh.somuch-system.ru/api/v1/push/vapid | grep -i access-control-allow-origin
#
# No header in that output means the deployment will not work, whatever it says
# here.
VITE_API_BASE=https://api-fh.somuch-system.ru/api/v1
VITE_WS_URL=wss://api-fh.somuch-system.ru/ws

# Only for a server that has no `GET /push/vapid`. The live one answers with a
# key, so this stays empty and push works without it.
VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development — read this before running `npm run dev`
#
# The values above do not work in development: the API does not allow
# `http://localhost:5173`, so the browser blocks every call from the dev server.
# There, the dev server proxies instead, which needs *relative* values. Put them
# in `.env.development`, which Vite loads only for `npm run dev` and which
# overrides `.env`:
#
#   VITE_API_BASE=/api/v1
#   VITE_WS_URL=/ws
#
# `vite.config.ts` forwards both paths to the API, which is also what keeps the
# page same-origin for the WebSocket upgrade. A relative WebSocket URL is valid
# in the browser — it resolves against the page and the scheme becomes `wss:`
# over https — so the same helper works in both modes.
#
# If you would rather develop against the deployed API directly, `localhost` has
# to be added to the server's allow-list first; otherwise a `dev`-mode build
# cannot reach it at all, however the two values are set.
